00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
good luck to those of you who are  planning something yeah good luck  or a victim of something all right  switching gears here uh  many of us have probably been spending  more time sitting on the couch  since the pandemic began and a new study  found that adults  have gained roughly one and a half  pounds every month since the start of  the lockdowns last year  and it's not only adults before the  pandemic one in three children in the  u.s were considered overweight or  obese and that number may have risen  with fewer opportunities for exercise we  spoke with one massachusetts mom  who told us she grew concerned that one  of her sons was much  less active when after school and other  activities  were shut down  before the pandemic miles was going to  karate every single day he was very  active in soccer  he also has brothers and so he was  always at the playground  millions of students across the country  log on from home  when covid hit mom had to say no more  karate  no more soccer it was for safety reasons  because  everyone was so fearful so our world got  really small  more screen time started happening we  were all trapped in the house and just  said hey  we are going to have pizza because it's  literally the only release that you have  that was kind of the breaking point for  me okay  time for lunch we started making some  changes cucumbers we're introducing a  lot more vegetables  miles was a part of that conversation  around  you know having healthier food limiting  snacks  making sure we had more exercise more  movement going on  we started to kind of take a look at our  neighborhood like were we more willing  to go down to the playground  and explore that a little bit because  they needed to start moving their bodies  it's not about the shape because people  come in all different shapes  but you got to feel comfortable i want  him to move his body and breathe fresh  air  and be a happy little kid with a sparkle  back  ordering that pizza is very relatable  cbs news senior medical correspondent dr  tara narula joins us now she's also a  cardiologist at northwell health in new  york uh dr narula good morning to you  so uh let's talk about the health risks  of obesity and then get into how parents  should talk about it  there was a recent study that found that  childhood obesity went up about 2  percent  during the pandemic so in the year 2020  what does that mean for kids health  well any parent will tell you tony that  this has been so difficult  to watch all the challenges our kids  have faced and many are calling this a  pandemic summer  we know during the summer kids tend to  gain weight and this is a summer that's  gone on for over a year  so certainly we know that kids who  develop obesity are at risk for becoming  adults who have obesity and there are  health risks associated for instance  hypertension high cholesterol and  diabetes  respiratory problems like asthma and  sleep apnea acid reflux  fatty liver joint and musculoskeletal  problems and then the whole host of  psychological issues that may come up as  well depression  anxiety and low self-esteem so it really  was the perfect storm of a pandemic  exacerbating an underlying epidemic of  childhood obesity in this country  yeah so so true story from my childhood  the the first  healthy body conversation i had involved  me getting out of the pool and my  stepfather just  pinching the fat on my belly part of  that moment  didn't even occur to me i had a belly  let alone that it could get bigger no so  i don't think that it would be the  recommended way to begin that  conversation  but how do you do it because it's a  delicate one and there are a lot of ways  to put your foot wrong  absolutely and you know we have two  young daughters my husband and i my  husband's a plastic surgeon  and so we really delicately walk around  these conversations  about bodies and really what we try to  do is steer away from  image and appearance and weight and  focus on healthy living and that's what  parents really want to do it  set the patterns the foundation for a  healthy lifestyle from a young age so  the same way  that we talk to our kids about finances  and teach them about money we teach them  about values and manners we help them  with their homework and goal setting  we need to be laying down healthy habits  for exercise and diet  so that they go off to college and they  know how to make the right choices and  really also we want to pay attention to  how our kids are feeling about their  weight and so  asking them if they come to us with  questions like am i overweight  really approaching that with well what  do you feel about your weight and  how can we help you as a family and  support you in terms of making good  choices  dr narula are there certain words that  parents should avoid when talking to  their kids about weight  well certainly again you you don't want  to put your kid on a diet that is  something that has been shown that can  actually increase the risk of eating  disorders and obesity  you also don't want to restrict certain  foods or have  one child able to eat certain foods in  the house and the other child  can't and then you want to be careful  about what you talk about  with your partner in the home around  your children so avoiding comments about  your own weight or if you see people on  television  saying comments about their weight or  their image because  kids will pick up on those cues so you  have to be careful about the  conversations you have  how much daily physical activity should  children have and adults too for that  matter during this time  so children should be getting about 60  minutes a day and for adults we usually  say 150 minutes of moderate  aerobic exercise per week which is like  30 minutes a day for adults  and really what you want to do both for  kids and adults is find activities that  you enjoy  doing a variety of activities and it's  been difficult with our kids because  they've been in the house but there are  things that kids can do at home  things like setting up an obstacle  course uh playing balloon volleyball we  call it balloon bop in my house  um and then there are dance and youtube  videos and games that kids can do go  noodle has a lot of great uh  educational videos for kids on how to  stay active cosmic yoga  so there there are things that parents  can offer their kids to keep them active  in fact  the american heart association has a  great app called nfl play 60

